From: RichardGeiger <rmg@netapp.com>
Date: Tue, 02 Feb 1999 08:37:06 -0800
Subject: Using Perforce for your web content?
Message-ID: <mailman.1897.1130361997.18943.perforce-user@perforce.com>


We use Perforce to manage several regions of our internal engineering
web pages.

Currently, use a cron job that syncs to the head of all the managed
pages (and page sources, since in some cases we use tools that build
the final HTML from "html sources"), and then runs the tools that
build the pages. This cron job runs every 15 minutes. I'm looking at
making it more intelligent - update daemon-based, perhaps.

We make sure that the whole site is done with relative links, so that
an individual's client workspace can be used for testing before
changes are submitted.

I'm trying to convince our external web (http://www.netapp.com) folks
that they would benefit greatly by using Perforce in a similar way.

I'm also toying with the idea of suggesting that my personal
ISP/webspace provider add Perforce client side support, so that I
could use Perforce for uploading my personal site. I use a "smart ftp"
client to do this now, but it's much less efficient that perforce
would be. Since they offer Solaris based shell access, this shouldn't
be amazingly difficult for them, though they do tend to be careful
about what binaries they allow to run on their hosts.

  - rmg
